.\" kenfsfind.1 - part of kenfs 1.0.0
.TH KENFSFIND 1 "August 2026" "kenfs 1.0.0" "File Format and Disk Utilities"
.SH NAME
kenfsfind \- locate filesystem superblocks (partitions) on a raw disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kenfsfind
.RB [ \-c
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Research Unix disk (V4 through 32V) is a set of
.I partitions
in one file, and the partition table is compiled into the kernel
.RB ( rp.c 's
.IR rp_sizes ,
.RB for example)
rather than stored on the disk.  So the offsets of the filesystems are not
readable from the disk itself.
.PP
.B kenfsfind
recovers them two ways.  First it scans every block (or every
.IR N -th
block) for a filesystem
.I superblock ,
validating the edition (V6, V7, or 32V), the i-list size, the volume size, and
that the free-block list holds only in-range block numbers.  Second, with
.BR \-i ,
it scans for runs of
.I inode-table
blocks and traces backwards: block 2 of a filesystem is its first inode-table
block, so the block just before an inode-table run is the superblock.  This
finds filesystems whose superblock is damaged enough to defeat the direct scan.
.PP
Each hit is printed as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fs @ block START  (byte BYTES)  V7  isize=N fsize=M
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mount the partition in place with
.BR kenfsmount (1)
using
.BR "\-o offset=BYTES" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c N
Scan every
.IR N -th
block instead of every block.  Use the disk's blocks-per-cylinder (418 for an
RP06, 400 for an RK05) to find partitions quickly and without the false
positives a block-by-block scan of file data produces.
.TP
.B \-i
Also trace inode-table runs backwards to their superblocks.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
kenfsfind -c 418 rp06-0.disk
# fs @ block 0      (byte 0)       V7  isize=202  fsize=5000
# fs @ block 18392  (byte 9416704) V7  isize=8189 fsize=322278

kenfsmount -v 7 -o offset=9416704 rp06-0.disk /mnt/usr
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kenfsmount (1),
.BR kenfs (5),
.BR icheck (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the kenfs project.
